From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] without a profile, ask for quicken rather than for all of it

Patch 0047 has the applications in /data compiled from their own usage profile
on the boot after a system update, and asks installd (frameworks/native patch
0006) for a different filter where there is no profile to compile from. That
filter was "speed", on the strength of Test V32: WeRead with no compiled code
took over two minutes to reach its bookshelf and put up an "isn't responding"
dialog.

Neither half of that holds now.

The measurement does not reproduce. Six paired rounds, the owner's own data,
the page cache dropped before every start, nothing compiled inside the loop:

  speed 37.1 s, quicken 41.0 s; paired difference +3.9 s, SD 3.4,
  t = 2.77 (df 5), p = 0.04, 95% confidence interval +0.3 to +7.5 s

and what the two cost to produce for WeRead 2.1.2 (70.4 MB of bytecode):
1214 s and 176 MB for "speed", 60 to 93 s and 76 MB for "quicken". A fallback
that spends twenty minutes of a device somebody has just updated and is waiting
to use, for a few seconds of a start that is about forty seconds either way, is
the wrong way round.

And the reason to have a fallback at all is not the one that was written down
here. "A profile filter with no profile compiles nothing" is about compiled
methods only: kSpeedProfile has IsQuickeningCompilationEnabled() true
(art/runtime/compiler_filter.cc), and a method ShouldCompileBasedOnProfile()
turns down is still passed to MarkForDexToDexCompilation()
(art/compiler/driver/compiler_driver.cc). The dex is quickened either way, so
the application is not left with nothing.

What the fallback buys is the time. Asking dex2oat to compile from a profile
that is not there, measured on this device with WeRead: 147 s, for a
1,057,192-byte odex and a 208,896-byte application image describing the classes
of a profile that is empty. Asking for "quicken": 62 s, for a 1,057,192-byte
odex and no application image. Same result, two and a half times the work.

So the flag keeps its job, changes its answer, and says so in its name:
DEXOPT_SPEED_IF_NO_PROFILE becomes DEXOPT_QUICKEN_IF_NO_PROFILE. The value is
unchanged; this half is the declaration in frameworks/base and its callers, and
frameworks/native patch 0008 is the other half.

